---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';

// Keep this page true to what the site actually does. If analytics, embeds,
// forms or hosting change, change this page in the same commit.
const UPDATED = 'November 13, 2026';
---
<PageLayout
  title="Privacy | The Caddie Chat"
  description="What The Caddie Chat collects when you read the site, why, and how to opt out."
  pageTitle="Privacy"
  pageSubtitle="What this site collects when you read it, why, and how to opt out."
  sectionLabel="The fine print"
>
  <section class="privacy">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="privacy-body">
        <p class="updated">Last updated {UPDATED}</p>

        <p>
          The Caddie Chat is a personal site written and run by Joe Stoehner. The stories,
          course pages and photo rolls have no comments, no newsletter sign-up and no ads.
          The one thing the site does collect is ordinary visit statistics, explained below.
        </p>

        <h2>Site statistics (Google Analytics)</h2>
        <p>
          The site uses Google Analytics 4 to count visits. It records which pages are
          read, roughly where readers are (country and city, worked out from the IP
          address, which Google Analytics does not store), what kind of device and browser
          was used, and which site or search sent the reader here. That includes whether a
          visit came from an AI assistant such as ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude, Gemini or
          Copilot.
        </p>
        <p>
          To tell one visit from another, Google Analytics sets first-party cookies named
          <code>_ga</code> and <code>_ga_6RST62EFLX</code>, which last up to two years. If
          you arrive from an AI assistant, the site also keeps a small note in your
          browser's session storage, cleared when you close the tab, so that visit is
          counted once.
        </p>
        <p>
          The statistics are used for one thing: to see what people read. They are not used
          for advertising, they are not sold, and the advertising features of Google
          Analytics are switched off. Visit-level data is kept for up to 14 months.
        </p>
        <p>
          Google processes this data on the site's behalf. Google's own explanation is at
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How Google uses information from sites that use its services</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Readers in Europe and the UK</h2>
        <p>
          If you are reading from the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom or
          Switzerland, the analytics cookies above are not set. Google Analytics receives a
          cookieless page count only, which cannot be tied to you from one page to the next.
        </p>

        <h2>How to opt out</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Install Google's <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Analytics opt-out add-on</a> for your browser.</li>
          <li>Block or clear cookies for thecaddiechat.com in your browser settings.</li>
          <li>Use a content blocker. The site works the same without analytics.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Hosting</h2>
        <p>
          The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. Like any web host, GitHub receives your IP
          address when your browser requests a page and may keep it in server logs for
          security. See
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
          Fonts and images are served from this site, not from third parties.
        </p>

        <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
        <p>
          Links to Instagram and other sites take you off The Caddie Chat. What those sites
          collect is covered by their own policies.
        </p>

        <h2>Questions</h2>
        <p>
          Message <a href="https://instagram.com/thecaddiechat" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@thecaddiechat</a>
          on Instagram. If this page changes, the date at the top changes with it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .privacy {
    padding: var(--space-lg) 0 var(--space-xl);
  }
  .privacy-body {
    max-width: 680px;
  }
  .privacy-body .updated {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .privacy-body h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--font-size-md);
    font-weight: 500;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 var(--space-sm);
  }
  .privacy-body p,
  .privacy-body li {
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .privacy-body ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  .privacy-body a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
  }
  .privacy-body code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>
